6:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed text-base","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"whitespace-pre-line","children":"Google recently released the Places API (New), also known as the v1 endpoints. This is a complete overhaul of the legacy Places API, offering better performance, cleaner schemas, and more precise pricing models."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"whitespace-pre-line","children":"If you are developing lead-generation utilities, here are the three critical concepts you must implement to build a production-grade system:"}],["$","p","2",{"className":"whitespace-pre-line","children":"1. X-Goog-FieldMask Header: In the new API, you must specify exactly which fields you want returned (e.g. `places.id,places.displayName`). If you omit a field, Google will not return it. Crucially, if you want pagination to work, you must include `nextPageToken` in your field mask."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"whitespace-pre-line","children":"2. pageToken Pagination: Unlike the legacy API which used offset numbers, the new API returns a hash string `nextPageToken`. In your next page request, you pass this token as `pageToken` in the JSON request body. Each page returns up to 20 results."}],["$","p","4",{"className":"whitespace-pre-line","children":"3. Concurrency Promise Pools: When resolving place details (like retrieving the Google Maps URI or specific website links), you must query place details individually for each ID. Running 50 sequential network requests is extremely slow, but running 50 parallel requests will trigger Google rate-limiting or exhaust system sockets. Implementing a concurrency limit of 5 using promise pools (like `p-limit`) ensures fast queries while remaining rate-safe."}]]}]
